.TH DIJO 1 "November 27, 2026" dijo-1.8.0

.SH NAME
dijo \- digital journal

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dijo
[\fBFLAGS\fR]
[\fBOPTIONS\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dijo
is scriptable, curses-based and modal, much like \fIvim\fR, digital habit tracker

.SH FLAGS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Prints help information
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List dijo habits
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Prints version information

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-missing " " \fIHABIT
Print the days in the current month a habit was not filled

.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.BR track " " \fIHABIT\fR " [" \fIAMOUNT\fR "] [" \-\-date " " \fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR "]"
Add \fIAMOUNT\fR (default 1) to the entry of \fIHABIT\fR for today or the given date, and save. A negative amount subtracts, and a float habit takes a decimal amount. A yes/no habit is set to yes, or to no with \-1. An amount of 0 records a clean day of a limit habit. Do not run it while the grid is open.
.TP
.BR status " [" \-\-json "]"
Print the progress of today, such as \fB3/5 done today\fR. With \fB\-\-json\fR, print JSON with \fBtext\fR, \fBtooltip\fR and \fBclass\fR (for a waybar custom module) and the details of each habit.

.SH FEATURES
.TP
\(bu \fBvim like motions\fR
Navigate with \fBhjkl\fR!
.TP
\(bu \fBdijo is modal\fR
Different modes to view different stats!
.TP
\(bu \fBvim like command mode\fR
Add with \fB:add\fR, delete with \fB:delete\fR and above all, quit with \fB:q!\fR.
.TP
\(bu \fBScriptable\fR
Query your habits from scripts with \fB\-\-list\fR and \fB\-\-missing\fR.

.SH GETTING STARTED
.PP
On running \fBdijo\fR at the command line, you are presented with a blank screen. This is the \fBDAY\fR mode, analogous to the \fBNORMAL\fR mode in Vim. In this mode, you may view stats for every day of the current month.
.PP
To start tracking habits, enter the command mode with \fB:.\fR Use the add command to add a habit to begin tracking.
.TP
Say, I would like to track the number of French lessons I took on Duolingo, and I aim to do 5 lessons each day:
.nf
\fB
:add french  5
       |     |
       `-----|----- habit name
              `---- goal (optional)
\fR
.fi
.TP
Go ahead and add a couple more habits the same way:
.nf
\fB
:add lifting 1   <-- a yes/no kind of habit (represented by a dot grid)
:add water       <-- no goal provided
:add smoke <5    <-- a limit: at most 5 a day (p on an empty day records a clean day)
\fR
.fi
.PP
To track your progress for the day:
.IP
\(bu focus the habit you want to track, (the focused habit is highlighted in bright white).
.IP
\(bu Hit \fBEnter\fR on the keyboard to increase the value, or \fBBackspace\fR to reduce it.
.IP
\(bu Each habit is a card with its own color. The top border of the card shows the name and the progress of today: a percentage, or a check mark once you reach the goal.
.PP
Cycle the display for every habit by pressing \fBv\fR on the keyboard. This steps through \fBDAY\fR, \fBWEEK\fR, \fBMONTH\fR, \fBSPARKLINE\fR, \fBYEAR\fR, \fBSTATS\fR and \fBHEATMAP\fR modes; press \fB<ESC>\fR to return to \fBDAY\fR mode and reset the cursor. The current mode is indicated on the status line.
.PP
\fBHEATMAP\fR mode draws a GitHub-style contribution grid (seven weekday rows by a band of trailing weeks) for each habit, shaded by completion. The rightmost column tracks the currently viewed week, so sifting months scrolls the heatmap through history.
.PP
Review your progress for previous months by pressing \fB[\fR on the keyboard, sift through months with \fB[\fR and \fB]\fR.
.PP
Press \fBd\fR (or run the \fB:dashboard\fR command) to open a full-screen dashboard for the focused habit: its all-time stats and a labelled, year-long contribution heatmap. Dismiss it with \fBq\fR or \fB<ESC>\fR.

.SH CUSTOMIZATION
.PP
\fBdijo (>= v0.2.7)\fR, can be configured via a configuration file. After its first run, \fBdijo\fR creates a configuration file. \fBdijo\fR must be restarted for changes in the configuration file to take effect.
.PP
This file is saved in different directories based on your operating system:

.nf
.IP
\(bu GNU/Linux: \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dijo/config.toml\fR
.IP
\(bu MacOS: \fB$HOME/Library/Application Support/rs.nerdypepper.dijo/config.toml\fR
.IP
\(bu Win10: \fB{FOLDERID_RoamingAppData}\[rs]nerdypepper\[rs]dijo\[rs]config\[rs]config.toml\fR
.fi

.PP
The default config file (created on first run) looks something like this, \fBconfig.toml\fR:
.IP
.nf
\fB
[look]
true_chr = \[dq]●\[dq]
false_chr = \[dq]✕\[dq]
future_chr = \[dq]·\[dq]
missing_chr = \[dq]○\[dq]
heat_chr = \[dq]■\[dq]

[colors]
reached = \[dq]#a6e3a1\[dq]
todo = \[dq]#f9e2af\[dq]
undone = \[dq]#f38ba8\[dq]
inactive = \[dq]light black\[dq]
accents = [\[dq]#89b4fa\[dq], \[dq]#a6e3a1\[dq], \[dq]#fab387\[dq], \[dq]#cba6f7\[dq], \[dq]#94e2d5\[dq], \[dq]#f5c2e7\[dq], \[dq]#f9e2af\[dq], \[dq]#f38ba8\[dq]]
\fR
.fi

.SS Look
.PP
Variables in this section define the characters \fBdijo\fR uses in \fBDAY\fR mode to represent days of the month. Every value in this section must span exactly 1 character in length.

.IP 
\(bu \fBtrue_chr\fR: The character to use in a Bit habit when the goal is \fBreached\fR
.IP 
\(bu \fBfalse_chr\fR: The character to use in a Bit habit when the goal is \fBnot reached\fR
.IP 
\(bu \fBfuture_chr\fR: The character to use for a day \fBin the future\fR.
.IP 
\(bu \fBmissing_chr\fR: The character to use for a past day that is \fBuntracked\fR.
.IP 
\(bu \fBheat_chr\fR: The character of one cell in \fBHEATMAP\fR mode and in the dashboard.

.SS Colors
.PP
Variables in this section define the colors \fBdijo\fR uses in all modes:

.IP 
\(bu \fBaccents\fR: A list of colors. Each habit takes one color from the list in turn, and uses it for its card, its reached days and its progress bars. A day with partial progress gets a darker shade of the same color.
.IP 
\(bu \fBreached\fR: The color of every habit when \fBaccents\fR is an empty list.
.IP 
\(bu \fBtodo\fR: The color to use when the goal is \fByet to be reached\fR and the habit color has no shades (a color that is not a hex value).
.IP 
\(bu \fBundone\fR: The color to use for a day that is \fBtracked but not done\fR: a Bit habit marked as not done, or a count set back to zero. An untracked day stays in the \fBinactive\fR color.
.IP 
\(bu \fBinactive\fR: The color to use for \fBunfocused cards, labels and untracked days\fR.

.PP
On a terminal that does not announce true color support (\fBCOLORTERM\fR is not \fBtruecolor\fR or \fB24bit\fR), \fBdijo\fR replaces each hex color with the nearest of the bright base colors.

.PP
The values in this section are strings, and may be populated by one of the following:
.IP
.nf
\fB
 black               light black
 red                 light red
 green               light green
 yellow              light yellow
 blue                light blue
 magenta             light magenta
 cyan                light cyan
 white               light white

 default  -- uses your terminal\[aq]s background color
 #123456  -- any color in hex (supported only on true color terminals)
\fR
.fi

.PP
\fBNote\fR: These values have to be quoted (ex.: \fBreached = \[dq]black\[dq]\fR)

.SH MODES
.PP
Press \fBv\fR to cycle the display for every habit through these views; press \fB<Esc>\fR to return to \fBDAY\fR mode:
.IP
\(bu \fBDAY\fR: every day of the current month
.IP
\(bu \fBWEEK\fR: weekly completion bars
.IP
\(bu \fBMONTH\fR: per-month completion for the year
.IP
\(bu \fBSPARKLINE\fR: the month in one row, with one bar per day
.IP
\(bu \fBYEAR\fR: per-year completion bars
.IP
\(bu \fBSTATS\fR: streaks, total completions and completion rate
.IP
\(bu \fBHEATMAP\fR: a contribution grid of trailing weeks
.SS \fBDAY\fR mode

.PP
\fBDAY\fR mode is the default mode for every habit. It shows the current month as a calendar, with one column for each day of the week from Monday to Sunday. In this mode, counting-type habits display their counts for each day of the month. Bit-type habits (yes/no) display \fBtrue_chr\fR or \fBfalse_chr\fR. Days whose goals have been reached are displayed in the color of the habit, and days with partial progress in a darker shade of it. Days that are tracked but not done are displayed in the \fBundone\fR color, and days that haven\[cq]t been tracked in the \fBinactive\fR color. Today is underlined and the day under the cursor is highlighted.

.SS \fBWEEK\fR mode
.PP
\fBWEEK\fR mode is one of the views cycled with \fBv\fR. It shows you a summary of your progress for every Monday-to-Sunday week that touches the month. The current week\[cq]s percentage is indicated in white, other weeks are colored in \[lq]light black\[rq]. The progress for a given week is calculated as follows:
.IP "1." 3
if the goal is reached for a day of the week, the day contributes
exactly 14.28% (100/7) towards that week\[cq]s progress.
.IP "2." 3
if the goal is not reached for a day of the week, the day contributes
\fBprogress / goal * 100\fR towards that week\[cq]s progress.
.PP
That means, to achieve a 100% for a week, you have to reach your goal
\fIevery single day of the week\fR.

.SS The Command mode
.PP
The command mode is different from the other modes in that it is a \[lq]control\[rq] mode rather than an \[lq]observe\[rq] mode. One can enter the command mode by hitting \fB:\fR on the keyboard. You will notice a \fB:\fR pop up at the bottom of the screen. You may now begin typing a command, once you are done, press \fBEnter\fR on the keyboard to execute it.
.PP
You can hit \fBTab\fR on the keyboard inside Command mode to
trigger completions. For example:
.IP
.nf
\fB
:d<Tab>
# completes to
:delete

:delete fr<Tab>
# completes to
:delete french
\fR
.fi

.SH COMMANDS
.SS Keybinds
.PP
These are key binds you can use at \fBDAY\fR or \fBWEEK\fR modes. Some of them are dependent on the currently focused habit and some are global. Those that are dependent on the currently focused habit are marked with
a \fB[f]\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Motions:
.RS 2
.nf
.IP \(bu 2
\fBh\fR - move one cell to the left (aliases: \fB<Tab>\fR, \fB<Arrow-Left>\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBj\fR - move one cell down (aliases: \fB<Arrow-Down>\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBk\fR - move one cell up (alases: \fB<Arrow-Up>\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBl\fR - move one cell to the right (aliases: \fB<Shift-Tab>\fR,
\fB<Arrow-Right>\fR)
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Editing
.RS 2
.nf
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<Enter>\fR - increment the currently focused habit (aliases: \fBn\fR) \fB[f]\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<Backspace>\fR - decrement the currently focused habit (aliases: \fBp\fR) \fB[f]\fR
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Modes
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\fBv\fR - cycle the view mode (\fBDAY\fR, \fBWEEK\fR, \fBMONTH\fR, \fBSPARKLINE\fR, \fBYEAR\fR, \fBSTATS\fR, \fBHEATMAP\fR) for all habits
.IP \(bu 2
\fBd\fR - open the focused habit\[cq]s dashboard \fB[f]\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBi\fR - show the focused habit\[cq]s full description in the message line \fB[f]\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<Esc>\fR - return to \fBDAY\fR mode
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Time Travel
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\fB[\fR - shift view port back by one month
.IP \(bu 2
\fB]\fR - shift view port forward by one month
.IP \(bu 2
\fB}\fR - return to present
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Control
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<Ctrl-c>\fR - quit without saving (subject to change)
.RE
.SS Commandline
.PP
Enter the command mode with \fB:\fR. Type out a command and press \fB<Enter>\fR on the keyboard to execute it. Most commands have `aliases', or short forms. Command mode supports auto-complete also.
.IP \(bu 2
Help: show command syntax
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Inputs: optional command or alias
.IP \(bu 2
Usage: \fBhelp [command]\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Example: \fB:help aa\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases: \fBh\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Add: add a habit
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Inputs: name of habit, optional daily goal
.IP \(bu 2
Usage: \fBadd <habit-name> [goal]\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Example: \fB:add french 5\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases: \fBa\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Describe: attach a one-line description shown under the habit name and in its dashboard
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Inputs: name of habit, description text (may contain spaces)
.IP \(bu 2
Usage: \fBdescribe <habit-name> <text...>\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Example: \fB:describe french practise for 10 minutes\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases: \fBdesc\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Delete: delete a habit
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Inputs: name of habit to delete
.IP \(bu 2
Usage: \fBdelete <habit-name>\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Example: \fB:delete water\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases: \fBd\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Rename: give a habit a new name; its archived months are renamed too
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Inputs: name of habit, new name
.IP \(bu 2
Usage: \fBrename <habit-name> <new-name>\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Example: \fB:rename gym lifting\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Goal: change the goal of a habit, in the same kind of habit (a count, a float or a limit; a yes/no habit has no goal to change)
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Inputs: name of habit, goal as in \fB:add\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Usage: \fBgoal <habit-name> <goal>\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Example: \fB:goal gym 4/week\fR, \fB:goal smoke <3\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Month motions: stats from the past
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Inputs: None
.IP \(bu 2
Usage: \fBmonth-prev\fR, \fBmonth-next\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Example: \fB:month-prev\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases: \fBmprev\fR, \fBmnext\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Archive: move past months out of the live record into per-month files
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Inputs: None
.IP \(bu 2
Usage: \fBarchive\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Example: \fB:archive\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Dashboard: open the focused habit\[cq]s dashboard
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Inputs: None
.IP \(bu 2
Usage: \fBdashboard\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Example: \fB:dashboard\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases: \fBdash\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Write: write progress to disk
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Inputs: None
.IP \(bu 2
Usage: \fBwrite\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Example: \fB:write\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases: \fBw\fR
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Quit: save and quit
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Inputs: None
.IP \(bu 2
Usage: \fBquit\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Example: \fB:quit\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases: \fBq\fR
.RE

.SH INTERNALS
.PP
This document delves into the internals of \fBdijo\fR.
.SS Files
.PP
After its first run, \fBdijo\fR creates two files on your file system: one to record habit data, \fBhabit_record.json\fR, and one to store the default configuration in, \fBconfig.toml\fR. Habit data is saved in a human-readable format, JSON; the config file is stored in TOML. The \fB:archive\fR command additionally writes per-month files under an \fBarchive\fR directory beside the habit record.

.SS Data files:
.PP
Making changes to these files while \fBdijo\fR is running, is not recommended (\fBdijo\fR will overwrite your changes on save).
.nf
.IP \(bu 2
GNU/Linux: \fB$XDG_DATA_HOME/dijo/*.json\fR
.IP \(bu 2
MacOS: \fB$HOME/Library/Application Support/rs.nerdypepper.dijo/*.json\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Win10: \fB{FOLDERID_RoamingAppData}\[rs]nerdypepper\[rs]dijo\[rs]data\[rs]*.json\fR
.fi

.SS Config files:
.PP
You can read more about configuring \fBdijo\fR at the Customization page.
.nf
.IP \(bu 2
GNU/Linux: \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dijo/config.toml\fR
.IP \(bu 2
MacOS: \fB$HOME/Library/Application Support/rs.nerdypepper.dijo/config.toml\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Win10: \fB{FOLDERID_RoamingAppData}\[rs]nerdypepper\[rs]dijo\[rs]config\[rs]config.toml\fR
.fi
.PP
\fBdijo\fR will not run on your computer if it can\[cq]t find your home directory.

.SS Data format
.PP
The general structure of a habit is as follows:
.IP
.nf
\fB
type  :: String,
name  :: String,
goal  :: HabitType,
stats :: Map<Date, HabitType>
\fR
.fi
.PP
\fBHabitType\fR is the type of data to be tracked and it is a \fBbool\fR for bit-type habits and a \fBu32\fR for counting habits.

